#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135109 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nFor example, consider the region $D$ below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d7573de141b9a640242644c64fc79a002d93b595fc0d05d1e9ad75317358cb3.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over $D,$ and\n\n$$\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 12, \\qquad \\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \\qquad \\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -2,$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is oriented counterclockwise, and $C_2,$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise. Let's use this information to calculate the value of the circulation $\\Gamma,$ given by\n\n$$\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.$$\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ab/ab13029d8b9168b384e14d2ae5f10d63c80b3fc8ca46695f59c2405443fa6b14.png)\n\nSince the value of each line integral is given for a positively oriented curve, we can calculate the total circulation over $D$ by summing the circulations over each boundary curve. Therefore,\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Γ & ={∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & =12+(-8)+(-2) \\\\ & =2.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "34167048-2745-5bf8-840c-3adfafb899fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5417 :tutorial/title "Calculating a Circulation Over a Region Containing Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135133 :tutorial/content "We now know how to calculate the circulation of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over a region $D$ that contains holes.\n\nIn previous lessons, we've seen how to use Green's theorem to calculate circulation. Often, Green's theorem allows us to convert a complex line integral over $\\partial D$ to a double integral over $D.$\n\nSo the question is, can we use Green's theorem to calculate the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ when $D$ contains holes?\n\nAs it turns out, the answer is yes. Moreover, we can show that applying Green's theorem to regions containing holes works the same way as with regions that don't have holes. We'll describe this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nWe can summarize Green's theorem to regions containing holes in a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNote that the region $D$ can contain a finite number of holes." :tutorial/id #uuid "50e81026-984c-52b1-8e07-d3af0248f817" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5846 :tutorial/title "Extending Green's Theorem to Regions With Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135134 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that we want to calculate the double integral\n\n$$\\iint_\\limits{D} f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65f622409e3fdd43f56dde79513e8693751465bb131dd828ce9f6cdfaa7d48aa.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ can be represented as the difference of sets $D = \\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 4, -2 \\leq y \\leq 3 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq x \\leq 3, 1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nIn such cases, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn other words, we can calculate a double integral over a region with a hole as the difference between two integrals." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d5a2750-408d-5d86-9d7b-2c95f46f262b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5847 :tutorial/title "A Note About Double Integrals Over Regions With Holes"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135138 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of a vector field $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of the region $D$ shown below.\n\n![](images/35/35a04c813c0b6922c6ee1a71997b9ca0c01a532dde274ca84165cfd97f511201.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is the region between the closed curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ and has a hole.\n\nWhen dealing with more complex regions, we usually write $\\partial D$ to refer to the boundary of $D.$ So, in this case, we have $$\\partial D = C_1 \\cup C_2.$$\n\nThe circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over the boundary of $D$ is simply the sum of the circulations over the two bounding curves:\n\n$$\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r + \\oint\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d\\mathbf r$$\n\nNotice that we have not yet determined the orientations of $C_1$ and $C_2.$ For this, we note the following important points:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise, and\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curve $C_2$ because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ clockwise.\n\nTherefore, to apply the above formula for the circulation, we need to choose our orientations as follows:\n\n![](images/54/5496ce5a457601608d5345f4176dc918b12ac090d5f8a77d8a834986352d3064.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c36205a2-23f5-5757-a4ad-9c15c9db94a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5858 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135139 :tutorial/content "To calculate the circulation of a vector field over a region $D$ containing holes, we add together the circulations over each positively oriented boundary. The outer curves must be oriented counterclockwise for this to work, whereas the inner curves must be oriented clockwise.\n\nTo make sense of why we need to choose our orientations in this way, we turn to Green's theorem.\n\nRecall that Green's theorem tells us that the macroscopic circulation over $\\partial D$ equals the sum of the scalar curls inside $D.$\n\n![](images/37/379238518375618b14a550ef571564ae4fa74238c9b112e80c559c6e54e74eec.png)\n\nNow, if we exaggerate the size of two of the curls inside $D,$ we get the following picture:\n\n![](images/5a/5aa0a1f7a3b021ac85984b8af188a83da5e4f990c4a188676a90ef6d1cf45a61.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- For the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *outer* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the outer curve must be oriented *counterclockwise*.\n- Similarly, for the curls' direction to match with the orientation of the *inner* curve at the points where they are in close contact, the inner curve must be oriented *clockwise*.\n\nSo, for Green's theorem to give us the correct answer, the inner and outer curves must be oriented clockwise and counterclockwise, respectively." :tutorial/id #uuid "1905ba8e-41ed-5562-9da9-28cae31e9c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5859 :tutorial/title "Intuition Behind the Choice of Orentations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135140 :tutorial/content "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral\n\n$$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented.\n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below\n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\,{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & +{{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r}_{⏟}}_{0} \\\\ & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\ & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6700e03f-e7d7-5f2c-88cb-17e9c69b7ed5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5860 :tutorial/title "An Illustration of How To Extend Green’s Theorem to Regions Containing Holes"}]}
